{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Pt5u195kKiJ9XRP7fxyFgZ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tScope of payload 28/32: .claude/scripts/check-installer-write-safety.py (now a facade over .claude/hooks/_lib/_installer_write_safety/), the new .claude/scripts/check-subprocess-tests.py (with .claude/scripts/_subprocess_census.py), .claude/scripts/check-substrate-drift.py (new CODEX_INSTALLED_COMPATIBLE branch) and .claude/scripts/check-substrate-watch.py (Codex currency arm). This was a static, read-only review: I read the code and executed nothing. Every trace below comes from reading the code.\n2\t\n3\tDECLARED CONDITIONS vs this payload\n4\t- Condition 7 holds here. The entry point keeps its path, and the logic now lives in _lib/_installer_write_safety. The condition makes no behavior-identity claim, so the finding below does not make it false.\n5\t- Condition 9 is consistent. The drift and watch changes follow from the version-floor policy. The extra downgrade case in the drift checker (P2-1) goes beyond the condition's wording, but it does not make the condition false.\n6\t- No declared condition is false against this payload. No P0 found.\n7\t- Python: standard-library only and 3.9-compatible. This covers FunctionType(code, globals, name, defaults, closure), the NamedTuple with a property, ast.dump show_empty gated on 3.13+, and (*KINDS, \"all\").\n8\t- Writes: none of these files writes anything, except the unchanged --write-baseline.\n9\t- check-subprocess-tests.py / _subprocess_census.py: no findings.\n10\t\n11\tNEW FINDINGS (annex)\n12\t\n13\tP1. .claude/hooks/_lib/_installer_write_safety/__init__.py:36 — the moved code breaks the exception hierarchy.\n14\t- Cause: _bind_class clones each package class with `type(original)(name, original.__bases__, attrs)`. The facade's Incomplete is therefore built on the ORIGINAL syntax.ParseError, not on the facade's cloned ParseError (syntax.py:6-11 defines `Incomplete(ParseError)`). In the facade, issubclass(Incomplete, ParseError) is False.\n15\t- Effect: every rebound handler `except ParseError` around a call that raises Incomplete no longer catches it. There are 8 such handlers: operands.py:35 (canon_operand), commands.py:131 (_expansion_refs), model.py:320 (extract_cmd_substs), model.py:344 (_subst_commands), model.py:632 (_token_is_expanded), and verdicts_b.py:141, 440, 447 (parse_sed_script, _sole_pipeline_producer).\n16\t- Failure scenario: a script under scripts/ contains `dd if=/dev/zero \"of=$dst\" bs=1 count=0`.\n17\t  - commands.py:518 builds the destination token from the raw text as `$dst\"`.\n18\t  - _record_occ calls canon_operand, which calls _read_dquote, which raises Incomplete. That exception escapes operands.py:35.\n19\t  - It then escapes build_file_model and run_census, which have no handler. The census dies with a traceback, exit 1, no --json output and no --write-baseline.\n20\t  - v1.4.3-rc.1 reported a write-candidate site for the same input.\n21\t- Same failure for a substitution body that lex cannot tokenise, e.g. `$(echo a`b)`. model.py:344 used to mark the file unparseable (a blocking parse site); now the census crashes.\n22\t- Not P0: the failure is still a nonzero exit, so CI fails closed, it is maintainer-only, and no file in today's scripts/*.sh corpus triggers it (grep for `\"of=` and a backtick inside `$(...)` found nothing).\n23\t- The test suite misses it: TestParseIsFailClosed (test_check_installer_write_safety.py:2189) only exercises a top-level ParseError.\n24\t- Honesty: this contradicts CHANGELOG [1.4.4] lines 112-113 (\"Entry points keep their paths and their observable contracts\") and the facade docstring.\n25\t- Minimal fix: in bind(), keep an original-to-clone map and build `bases = tuple(clones.get(b, b) for b in original.__bases__)`. syntax is bound before the others, so ParseError is cloned first. Alternatively, do not clone BaseException subclasses. Add a test for `issubclass(mod.Incomplete, mod.ParseError)` and a `dd \"of=$x\"` fixture.\n26\t\n27\tP2 FOLLOW-UPS\n28\t\n29\tP2-1. .claude/scripts/check-substrate-drift.py:571-578 — the new \"compatible\" branch uses the pin file's floor, not the pair-rail's acceptance rule.\n30\t- The pin file's floor is `>=0.128.0` (.claude/governance/codex-cli-pin.txt:199). The pair-rail accepts only a version that is ≥ CODEX_VERSION_FLOOR 0.160.0 AND newer than the manifest version (cli_currency.compatible_package_version via _pair_rail_parts/part_01.py:566).\n31\t- Scenario: installed Codex is between 0.128.0 and 0.159.x, e.g. the previous pin 0.156.1.\n32\t  - The drift checker reports info CODEX_INSTALLED_COMPATIBLE, `--strict` exits 0, and the \"restore the pinned binary\" advice disappears.\n33\t  - Meanwhile `--verify-codex-pin` returns mismatch, and the pair-rail fails closed (part_01.py:587).\n34\t- The message \"the payload hash is verified separately\" also misleads: on the compatible route, a hash mismatch is tolerated.\n35\t- The module docstring at lines 10-14 is now stale.\n36\t- Maintainer-only: installs have no .claude/governance, so the check returns \"absent\" (lines 388-391).\n37\t- Fix: take the info branch only when `cli_currency.compare_semver(installed, max(CODEX_VERSION_FLOOR, pinned)) > 0`; otherwise fall through to CODEX_INSTALLED_NOT_PINNED. Reword the message to \"version floor only; no manifest attests these bytes\".\n38\t\n39\tP2-2. .claude/scripts/check-substrate-watch.py:237-250 — Codex drift alarms are silenced at or above the floor.\n40\t- For both codex_cli and codex_harness, any installed version ≥ 0.160.0 suppresses drift and the fixture re-record runbook (lines 112-129).\n41\t- This includes Codex as a hook host, whose fixtures still date from 0.139.0 (CHANGELOG Known-open, line 396). Nightly hygiene emits findings only for drift=true rows, so that alarm is lost.\n42\t- The summary \"installed matches last_seen for all components\" (line 269) becomes false: test_cli_floor_consumers.py:53-63 asserts status \"current\" with 0.166.0 installed against 0.160.0 last seen.\n43\t- Docstring lines 21-24 and 41-47 are stale, and the CHANGELOG does not declare this change.\n44\t- The two instruments also disagree on 0.128-0.159: the watch reports drift, the drift checker reports compatible.\n45\t- Fix: apply the currency arm to codex_cli only, or keep drift=True for codex_harness; correct the summary text.\n46\t\n47\tP2-3. .claude/scripts/check-substrate-watch.py:67 — import failure now crashes a script documented as fail-open.\n48\t- The module-level `from _lib import cli_currency` is unguarded. A missing _lib or an import error now crashes with exit 1 and a traceback.\n49\t- The docstring (lines 34-36) promises fail-open with exit 0 on infrastructure faults. Existing callers tolerate the crash (validate.yml:209-213 turns it into a warning).\n50\t- Fix: wrap the import in try/except ImportError and treat Codex as not current.\n51\t\n52\tP2-4. .claude/hooks/_lib/_installer_write_safety/__init__.py:29-35 — property objects are not rebound.\n53\t- Site.blocking (report.py:32-34) still reads BLOCKING_VERDICTS from the report module, not from the facade.\n54\t- A harness that patches the facade's BLOCKING_VERDICTS therefore does not affect it, contrary to the \"one patchable facade namespace\" promise.\n55\t- Fix: rebind property fget/fset/fdel the same way methods are rebound.\n56\t\n57\tP2-5 (cosmetic). verdicts_b.py:698 — `__all__` excludes `_engine` and `_engine_name` but still exports `_commands` (a module object) and `_commands_name` into the facade namespace.\n58\t\n59\tVERDICT: GO-WITH-CONDITIONS No declared condition is false against this payload and there is no P0, but the facade's rebinding breaks the Incomplete/ParseError hierarchy: the installer census now crashes (still exiting nonzero, so CI fails closed) on inputs it used to report as sites, which needs to be carried as a signed annex P1 and fixed before GA, alongside the substrate-drift and watch P2s.\n60\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e9f21750-77cd-44a8-9a48-5376b7294476","uuid":"abaeb95d-0b86-47b2-a368-e66e30aca4ef","timestamp":"2026-11-16T20:00:45.420Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.n5R3O9/tree/.claude/plans/PLAN-198/repass-rc1/claude-rc1-28.verdict.txt","content":"Scope of payload 28/32: .claude/scripts/check-installer-write-safety.py (now a facade over .claude/hooks/_lib/_installer_write_safety/), the new .claude/scripts/check-subprocess-tests.py (with .claude/scripts/_subprocess_census.py), .claude/scripts/check-substrate-drift.py (new CODEX_INSTALLED_COMPATIBLE branch) and .claude/scripts/check-substrate-watch.py (Codex currency arm). This was a static, read-only review: I read the code and executed nothing. Every trace below comes from reading the code.\n\nDECLARED CONDITIONS vs this payload\n- Condition 7 holds here. The entry point keeps its path, and the logic now lives in _lib/_installer_write_safety. The condition makes no behavior-identity claim, so the finding below does not make it false.\n- Condition 9 is consistent. The drift and watch changes follow from the version-floor policy. The extra downgrade case in the drift checker (P2-1) goes beyond the condition's wording, but it does not make the condition false.\n- No declared condition is false against this payload. No P0 found.\n- Python: standard-library only and 3.9-compatible. This covers FunctionType(code, globals, name, defaults, closure), the NamedTuple with a property, ast.dump show_empty gated on 3.13+, and (*KINDS, \"all\").\n- Writes: none of these files writes anything, except the unchanged --write-baseline.\n- check-subprocess-tests.py / _subprocess_census.py: no findings.\n\nNEW FINDINGS (annex)\n\nP1. .claude/hooks/_lib/_installer_write_safety/__init__.py:36 — the moved code breaks the exception hierarchy.\n- Cause: _bind_class clones each package class with `typ